            {
                "entry_id": "c017d302-790d-3d54-94aa-530258ccace0",
                "word": "stang",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "404800f5-bc43-40ee-87d0-018a99ddcadd",
                        "entry_id": "c017d302-790d-3d54-94aa-530258ccace0",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "stangs"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "de46156a-97f5-34cd-91b7-343969cde838",
                        "entry_id": "c017d302-790d-3d54-94aa-530258ccace0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A forked ritual staff."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e112e992-61b9-432f-9e89-18f79e760cb4",
                                "sense_id": "de46156a-97f5-34cd-91b7-343969cde838",
                                "text": "The stang represents the horned god and the masculine force of nature. Much like the wand or athame, the stang is used for raising or directing power[…]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e11b2643-4b55-4c65-964f-a56225bfec7c",
                                "sense_id": "de46156a-97f5-34cd-91b7-343969cde838",
                                "text": "In more common (and private) Wiccan circles, the stang is replaced by the athame (black handled knife) and the cauldron replaced with the chalice but its^([sic]) pretty much /whatever works for you/."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "186ff64f-7125-49dc-a946-edb6b26e7579",
                                "sense_id": "de46156a-97f5-34cd-91b7-343969cde838",
                                "text": "These stangs can be used as natural altars in outdoor rituals or simply as walking staffs."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "03399799-ac61-369d-9695-5f4352c8dbb9",
                        "entry_id": "c017d302-790d-3d54-94aa-530258ccace0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A long bar; a pole; a shaft; a stake."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d18d1bd8-5b15-4d00-b0d8-f5b9c1f1395b",
                                "sense_id": "03399799-ac61-369d-9695-5f4352c8dbb9",
                                "text": "Gripping the stang, she peered / At ghostly trees. Bus stopped. Bus disappeared."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "79782f20-b5aa-3f40-9d9d-93ec88a52ca3",
                        "entry_id": "c017d302-790d-3d54-94aa-530258ccace0",
                        "glosses": [
                            "In land measure, a pole, rod, or perch."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cfb6cb21-0d0f-457a-91cc-df6db6403a80",
                                "sense_id": "79782f20-b5aa-3f40-9d9d-93ec88a52ca3",
                                "text": "These fields were intermingled with woods of half a stang,*... (with the corresponding footnote: \"An old word for a perch, sixteen feet and a half. These small woods were therefore eight feet and a quarter.\")"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
